 - ToolSearch: {"query": "select:mcp__tool__bus_factor,mcp__tool__knowledge_risk,mcp__tool__repo_summary,mcp__tool__blame_summary", "max_results": 4}
 - mcp__tool__repo_summary: {"repo_path": "<home>/claude-kredi-runs/2026-10-22/r8/out-D/runs/D2-hist-2/repo"}
 - mcp__tool__knowledge_risk: {"repo_path": "<home>/claude-kredi-runs/2026-10-22/r8/out-D/runs/D2-hist-2/repo", "top": 30, "max_files": 500}
 - Bash: git ls-files '*.go' | grep -v '^vendor/' | grep -v '^zz_generated' > /tmp/gofiles.txt; wc -l < /tmp/gofiles.txt; for f in $(cat /tmp/gofiles.txt); do git blame -w -M -C --line-porcelain HEAD -- "$f" | grep '^author ' ; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | awk '{c=$1; $1=""; t+=c; a[NR]=c; n[NR]=$0} END{cum=0; for(i=1;i<=NR&&i<=15;i++){cum+=a[i]; printf "%5d %5.1f%% cum %5.1f%% %s\n",a[i],100*a[i]/t,100*cum/t,n[i]} print "total",t,"authors",NR}'; git log -1 --format=%cd; git log --since='2025-10-22' --no-merges --format='%an %ad %s' --date=short | head -20
FINAL ANSWER:
Surviving Go code is spread thin across many authors, but almost none of them are still active. Losing one or two people is not the problem. The bigger problem is that nearly all the knowledge is already gone.

**Scope.** I counted only hand-written Go, using `git blame` at HEAD. That is 75 files and 12,194 lines. I excluded `vendor/` and `zz_generated_flags.go`. The `git-historian` tool counts those, which makes Sam Okafor look like the owner of 4 files and 11% of the lines. Okafor's commits are vendoring, regeneration and the `interface{}` to `any` rewrite. Okafor owns none of the hand-written logic.

**Who owns the live Go lines** (79 authors in total):

| Author | Share | Cumulative | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Eric Paris | 16.6% | 16.6% | 2018 |
| Alex Ogier | 10.1% | 26.7% | 2015 |
| Noah Dietz | 10.1% | 36.8% | 2018 |
| Jonathan Reyna | 8.6% | 45.3% | 2017 |
| Tamal Saha | 5.1% | 50.5% | 2018 |
| Riobard | 4.4% | 54.9% | 2014 |
| therealmitchconnors | 4.3% | 59.2% | 2019 |
| Georges Varouchas | 3.7% | 62.9% | 2025 |

- **Core files.** Paris and Ogier together own about 40% of `flag.go`. They also own about 48% of `flag_test.go`, and neither has committed since 2018 or earlier.
- **Slice and map types.** Dietz owns the int, float and int64 slice types. Reyna owns the bool, ip and uint slice types. Saha owns the `string_to_*` types.
- **Recent code.** `errors.go` is from Ethan P. in 2025. `flagset_helpers.go` is 93% Lena Voss, from March 2026.

**Concentration.** It is low. The top 5 authors cover about 50% of the lines, and it takes 15 authors to reach about 80%. No file depends on one active person. Losing any one or two people would remove at most about 27% of the lines, and Paris and Ogier are already gone.

**The real risk:**
- The last commit is 2026-04-08. Every author has been inactive for more than 6 months, so the tool flags all 93 files as orphaned.
- Most of the code is from authors whose last commit was 2014 to 2020. Nobody who wrote the core parsing, the slice types or the tests is around to ask.
- Recent activity is thin: 5 non-merge commits in the last 12 months, and the only hand-written feature was the `Require*` helpers by Voss.
- That makes you the main reviewer by default, so you will need to read `flag.go` and `flag_test.go` carefully yourself.

My blame run did not apply `.mailmap`, so an author with two spellings may be split across two rows. That would shift the percentages slightly but not the overall picture.

