FINAL ANSWER:
Method: `git blame -w -M` on 76 non-vendor Go files, with `--ignore-rev 6bf5b04` so the `interface{}` → `any` rewrite doesn't count. I excluded `zz_generated_flags.go` (488 lines, all Sam Okafor, regenerated by tooling) and vendored code. Live lines: 12,194 including tests, 6,397 excluding tests. 77 distinct authors, with `bogem` and `therealmitchconnors` merged into Albert Nigmatzianov and Mitch Connors.

**Top authors by surviving lines (including tests)**

| Author | Share | Cumulative |
|---|---|---|
| Eric Paris | 16.7% | 16.7% |
| Noah Dietz | 10.1% | 26.8% |
| Jonathan Reyna | 8.6% | 35.4% |
| Riobard | 7.6% | 42.9% |
| Alex Ogier | 6.9% | 49.8% |
| Tamal Saha | 5.1% | 54.9% |
| Mitch Connors | 4.3% | 59.2% |
| wu8685 | 3.3% | 62.5% |

Excluding tests: Eric Paris 16.5%, Riobard 14.4%, Noah Dietz 8.1%, Jonathan Reyna 6.6%, Alex Ogier 6.5%, Mitch Connors 6.0%, Tamal Saha 4.8%. Together the top 7 hold 63%.

**Is it concentrated?**
- Only mildly. No one holds more than about 17%, and 77 people contributed. Eric Paris is the largest single holder.
- Losing one or two people loses about 25–30% of the live lines. That is a knowledge risk but not a single point of failure.
- Line ownership is not the same as who can review today. Almost all the big holders are historical contributors. By commit dates in the log:
  - Eric Paris: 103 commits, 2015–2018.
  - Alex Ogier: 37 commits, 2012–2015.
  - Noah Dietz, Tamal Saha, Jonathan Reyna, Riobard and wu8685 each made 1–2 commits, years ago. Large one-off changes account for their line counts.
- The people active recently are a different group:
  - Lena Voss wrote the latest feature (#512, `Require*` helpers). That is about 143 live non-test lines.
  - Sam Okafor wrote the generated accessors and made the `any` refactor.
  - Tomas Aschan has the most commits in the last 2 years (18). Those commits are small, so they hold few surviving lines.
  - Hu Jun, Georges Varouchas, Ethan P., Sebastiaan van Stijn and Maximilian Frank also have commits in the last 2 years.

**Practical read**
- The risk is not "lose one or two people". The code mostly belongs to people who have already left, so you probably inherit that gap now.
- Current knowledge sits with Lena Voss, Sam Okafor and Tomas Aschan. For the original design, such as flag-set parsing and pflag's core files, nobody active holds much. Expect to rely on tests and git history there.
- If you want, I can break ownership down per file (for example `flag.go`, `string_slice.go`) so you know who to ask or tag for each area.

